# nag.env — copy this file to nag.env, then append the generated keys:
#
#   cp nag.env.example nag.env
#   docker compose run --rm -T nag genkeys >> nag.env
#
# This template declares no variables on purpose. genkeys appends the four
# real lines below; a template carrying NAG_TOKEN= with an empty value would
# leave two of every key, and the one hand edit would then have two
# candidate lines.
#
# Required — all four are written by genkeys:
#
#   NAG_TOKEN           bearer token, also the cookie signing key.
#                       At least 20 characters. Rotating it logs out every
#                       browser.
#   NAG_VAPID_PUBLIC    Web Push identity: unpadded base64url, 65 bytes.
#   NAG_VAPID_PRIVATE   Web Push identity: unpadded base64url, 32 bytes.
#                       Never rotate the pair casually: every browser must
#                       open the app once to re-subscribe.
#   NAG_VAPID_SUBJECT   mailto: or https: contact for push services.
#                       genkeys writes mailto:you@example.com, and nag
#                       refuses to start until you replace it with your own
#                       address. This is the one hand edit.
#
# Optional — leave unset for the defaults, which match compose.yaml:
#
#   NAG_DB              SQLite database path. Default /data/nag.db
#   NAG_CONFIG          TOML config path. Default /config/nag.toml
#                       (written from the built-in default on first boot)
#   NAG_ADDR            listen address. Default :8080
